NOTE: 

sphere_post is the post_processing program for sphere2--sphere5, sphere9 etc.
streamfunction is for sphere2--sphere5, sphere9 etc.

The examples in this directory are described in the userguide, except for
sphere9a


Short description:
-----------------

sphere1:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).

sphere2:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 first-order time integration

sphere3:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 first-order time integration

sphere4:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS/DG
 first-order time integration

sphere5:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS/DG
 second-order time integration (AB2)

sphere6:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 Drag computations. 
 This problem is identical to sphere1 + drag

sphere9:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 first-order time integration
 triangles
 This example is similar to sphere2, but uses triangles instead of quads.

sphere9a:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 first-order time integration
 triangles
 This is the sphere9 problem with the following changes:
  1) P1isoP2 shape functions
  2) subdomain Gauss integration (using 4 subtriangles)

sphere13:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

 Identical to sphere3, but now using the sparskit solver with metis renumbering

sphere21:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

sphere22:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration
 NOTE: this is similar to sphere21, but now using the L2-projection of
       c=exp(s). 

sphere23:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the cylinder wall is moved with velocity -U 
 (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity -U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom). At the right inflow wall the stress is
 assumed to be zero.
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 second-order time integration of the CE using a Gear/conformation
 time prediction scheme 
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 To increase the time step limit, c/lambda has been added to both sides of 
 the discretized CE and this term in the LHS is treated implicitly, while 
 it is treated explicitly on the RHS
 two viscoelastic modes

sphere24:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the cylinder wall is moved with velocity -U 
 (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity -U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom). At the right inflow wall the stress is
 assumed to be zero.
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 second-order time integration of the CE using a Gear/conformation
 time prediction scheme 
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 To increase the time step limit, c/lambda has been added to both sides of 
 the discretized CE and this term in the LHS is treated implicitly, while 
 it is treated explicitly on the RHS
 two viscoelastic modes

 Similar to sphere23, but using a four mode Giesekus model.

sphere25:

 3D Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity -U on the center
 line of a container with a square cross section and a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the container wall is moved in z-direction with
 velocity U (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Drag computations. 
 Sparskit solver
 Metis renumbering
 Gmsh mesh with tetrahedrons.

sphere26:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

 Similar to sphere22, but now using an XPP model.

sphere27:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

 Similar to sphere22, but now using an elastoviscoplastic model.

sphere28:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

 Similar to sphere27, but now using a CDT formulation.

sphere29:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).

 This problem is similar to sphere1, but now with high-order elements having
 equal-order continuous stabilized velocity/pressure Qp/Qp quadrilateral
 elements 

sphere30:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).

 This problem is similar to sphere1, but now with high-order elements having
 equal-order continuous stabilized velocity/pressure Pp/Pp triangular elements 

sphere31:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).

 This problem is similar to sphere1, but now using blended meshes with
 both biquadratic and bilinear quadrilateral elements.
 
sphere32:

 Axisymmetrical Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the center
 line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 High-order Taylor-Hood quadrilateral elements using blended meshes.

 This problem is similar to sphere1, but now with high-order Taylor-Hood
 velocity/pressure Qp/Qp-1 elements using blended meshes.

sphere33:

 Axisymmetrical viscoelastic problem: a sphere moving with velocity U on the
 center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom (at infinity).
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 second-order time integration

 Similar to sphere3, but now using the UMFPACK solver.

sphere34:

 Axisymmetrical generalized Newtonian problem: a sphere moving with
 velocity U on the center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom
 The sphere is moving with velocity U. At the ends fluid is at rest 
 (zero velocity) representing a bottom.
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Picard iteration followed by Newton-Raphson iteration.
 All models can be tested, including the yield models.

sphere35:

 Axisymmetrical generalized Newtonian problem: a sphere moving with
 on the center line of a cylindrical container with a bottom
 (at infinity) due to an applied force F. 
 At the ends fluid is not moving, representing a bottom.
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Picard iteration followed by Newton-Raphson iteration.
 All models can be tested, including the yield models.

sphere36:

 3D Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity -U on the center
 line of a container with a square cross section and a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the container wall is moved in z-direction with
 velocity U (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Drag computations. 

sphere37:

 3D Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity -U on the center
 line of a container with a square cross section and a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the container wall is moved in z-direction with
 velocity U (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Drag computations. 
 Mkl_gmres solver
 Metis renumbering

sphere38:

 3D Stokes problem: a sphere moving with velocity -U on the center
 line of a container with a square cross section and a bottom (at infinity).
 The sphere is fixed and the container wall is moved in z-direction with
 velocity U (moving frame).
 At the ends fluid is moving with velocity U as well, representing zero
 flux in the stationary frame (bottom).
 Dirichlet boundary conditions for the velocities are used.
 Drag computations. 
 P2+/P1^d tetrahedrons
